{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:848db64a467f59ed6a5d60ddfa96c37f0bddb077d99f68097954960c47bad5cf","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.8},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.7}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PPP WEIGHT RECONCILIATION FIRST. China 2025 PPP GDP ~$41T; OECD-38 ~$78T (US 30.5, Japan 6.7, Germany 6.1, UK 4.4, France 4.4, Italy 3.5, Turkiye 3.5, Korea 3.3, Mexico 3.3, Canada 2.6, Spain 2.7, Poland 1.9, Australia 1.9, rest ~3.7). Combined $119T -> China 0.345, OECD-38 0.655. I use 0.66/0.34 and the weights are FROZEN at 2025 per the accounting lock, which lowers the composite ~5-10bp versus the IMF's own chain-weighted current-PPP practice. The Reference-Class panelist's 0.30 China weight is 4pp too low and the Auditor's 0.36 is 1.5pp too high. OECD-38 GROWTH-ACCOUNTING SUM, 2032-36: labour input = employment -0.10%/yr (20-64 headcount -0.35, migration +0.20, retirement-age extension +0.15, participation +0.10 -> net ~ -0.10) x labour share 0.62 = -0.06pp; labour quality (education share) +0.15 x 0.62 = +0.09pp; subtotal labour +0.03pp. Capital deepening = capital services +1.80%/yr x capital share 0.38 = +0.68pp, marked to +0.65pp because 2032-36 sits AFTER the AI/grid buildout peak (the AI-Q3 implied capex is only $35-75B/yr against OECD-38+China power capex of $600-800B/yr, 5-10%, so the AI capex stock is not a large enough term to lift the contribution in the measurement window and the AI-Q4 66% impairment marginal implies flow retrenchment). TFP = 2015-22 OECD baseline +0.25pp, plus the AI-Q6-implied expected lift 0.162(0.70) + 0.472(0.35) + 0.365(0.05) = 0.113 + 0.165 + 0.018 = +0.30pp, minus the Baumol reallocation drag -0.13pp (3.5pp of hours over 11 years = 0.32pp/yr shifting into a sector at 0.6x average output per hour: 0.0032 x (0.6-1.0) = -0.13pp/yr; this independently reproduces the Mechanism panelist's -0.15 to -0.20), plus the healthy-aging attribution leakage +0.10pp (the Auditor's point: \"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000\" is unmeasured labour quality and lands in the Solow residual) = +0.52pp. Frictions (interconnection queue, care rationing) -0.05pp. OECD-38 = 0.03 + 0.65 + 0.52 - 0.05 = 1.15%. CROSS-CHECK against 2A directly: per-capita 0.60 + population 0.25 = 0.85 baseline, +0.30 AI, -0.13 Baumol = 1.02%. Two routes give 1.02-1.15 -> OECD P50 = 1.10%. CHINA, 2032-36 REPORTED: labour force -1.30%/yr x labour share 0.52 = -0.68pp, plus urbanization reallocation +0.30 and quality +0.20 = -0.18pp; capital = capital services +4.2%/yr x share 0.48 = +2.02pp; TFP +1.00pp (0.70 trend plus 0.30 automation substitution under labour scarcity). True = -0.18 + 2.02 + 1.00 = 2.84%. CQ3 resolves on NBS-reported real GDP as reprinted by the IMF, and the reported-versus-deflator-consistent wedge is ~+0.5 to +0.8pp (Auditor's point, Rhodium-class evidence) -> China reported P50 = 3.10%. WEIGHTED COMPOSITE = 0.66(1.10) + 0.34(3.10) = 0.726 + 1.054 = 1.78 -> P50 1.8%. THE WEIGHT/DRAG INTERACTION THE LENS REQUIRES, AND IT CLOSES AN IDENTITY: 0.66 x (-0.4pp OECD aging drag, i.e. 1.0 -> 0.6 per capita) + 0.34 x (-2.7pp China aging drag) = -0.264 - 0.918 = -1.18pp, against 2A's stated global -1.1pp. The two agree to 8bp, which PROVES the -1.1 is already NET of the +0.4pp healthy-aging offset: if the +0.4 were additive the implied per-region drags would be -0.4 and -2.3 summing to -0.78pp, contradicting 2A's own figures. Any effort that adds +0.4 to -1.1 inflates coupled growth by ~0.4pp. P10 = 1.0%: one recession year in the window plus a harder China landing gives 0.66(0.30) + 0.34(2.00) = 0.198 + 0.680 = 0.88, and a five-YEAR average that low is a tail, so 1.0. P90 = 2.7%: AI TFP at the top of the AI-Q6 band (+0.60) with migration clearing the care constraint (no Baumol drag) gives OECD 1.80, China reflating to 4.00 -> 0.66(1.80) + 0.34(4.00) = 1.188 + 1.360 = 2.55, plus ~15bp of chain-weight/reporting slack = 2.70. Left-skewed: the weakest-link care/grid structure caps the upside while the fiscal-financial channel has no symmetric floor. Implied sigma = (2.70-1.00)/2.563 = 0.66pp.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.589Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey 2030 wave: real median gross hourly earnings growth for NACE F+Q minus J+K+M over the 2022-2030 interval, EU-OECD members, published ~2032-33.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"A real gap above +6pp over eight years falsifies my composition wedge of -8.0pp (it would imply the wedge is nearer -3pp), moving CQ1 from 13 to ~21 and hypothesis (a) from 3 to ~10. A gap below +1pp, or a negative gap, confirms the wedge and moves CQ1 to ~8 and hypothesis (a) to 2.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 PPP-GDP-weighted core inflation for calendar year 2029, as published on OECD.Stat in early 2030, with the Turkiye and Mexico country components reported separately.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"A print below 2.2% falsifies the composition-floor term that carries my answers: CQ2 falls from 46 to ~32, CQ6-C from 33 to ~22 with the mass going to B and D (B ~50, D ~17), and my coupling_tension claim about AI-Q5 weakens. A print above 3.0% moves CQ2 to ~55 and CQ6-C to ~40.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD harmonized unemployment rate, 12-month average for calendar 2031 (OECD MEI / LFS).\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Above 6.0% falsifies my 5.00% no-recession central path and the -0.30pp denominator effect: CQ1's first conjunct drops from 0.58 to ~0.25 and CQ1 to ~6, while CQ3's P10 drops to 0.7%. Below 5.0% lifts the conjunct to ~0.72 and CQ1 to ~16.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 annual inflows of foreign-trained nurses and personal-care workers for calendar 2030 (OECD International Migration Outlook, 2032 edition), plus Philippine BPO headcount for FY2031 (IBPAP annual).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Care inflows above 900k/yr falsify the 400-700k/yr entry-capacity ceiling I used: the Baumol drag of -0.13pp/yr shrinks toward -0.05, CQ3 P50 rises to ~2.0%, CQ6-C falls ~8pp into B, and hypothesis (b) falls to ~7. Philippine BPO headcount above 2.1M (versus ~1.7M) independently falsifies the virtual-leg compression and takes hypothesis (b) to ~6.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US 10-year TIPS yield (FRED DFII10) average for calendar 2031, together with the G7 PPP-GDP-weighted nominal-minus-breakeven aggregate as reconstructible from national index-linked markets.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"A US average above 3.0% with the G7 aggregate above 2.4% falsifies my -27bp r* term and takes CQ4 P50 to ~2.5%, CQ5 to ~34 (the MOVE floor shifts to ~100), and CQ6-C up ~6pp. A US average below 1.5% with the aggregate below 1.2% falsifies the +80bp term-premium term, taking CQ4 P50 to ~1.4%, CQ5 to ~18, and CQ6-D up ~6pp.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 fermi seat, effort 'labor'."}}